# answered by claude-opus-5-5, prompt /lib/phil/senses f4c9d13865f3, 2026-10-19
sensible	one
perceived_by_sense	two	perceive	P3	what we see, hear or touch in the ordinary way, such as the house itself	what is immediately present to the mind in perceiving, i.e. the content of sensation
idea	one
exists_unperceived	one
opinion_contradictory	one
# perceived_by_sense: P2 holds because houses and rivers are what we ordinarily perceive. P3 holds only if "perceive" means immediate awareness, whose objects are ideas.
# On the ordinary sense, P3 is false, since we perceive houses and not only ideas. This is the standard objection to this passage.
